 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "A coil of silver conductor runs through stacked layers of Ni-Cu-Zn ferrite. Each end carries a silver termination with a nickel barrier and tin finish."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Material grades",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The letter after the size code is the ferrite grade, and it follows the value: V for 0.047 µH to 0.82 µH, U for 1.0 µH to 4.7 µH, X for 5.6 µH to 12 µH and J for 15 µH to 68 µH. The Q test frequency falls with value: 50 MHz up to 0.082 µH, 25 MHz for the rest of V, 10 MHz for U, 4 MHz or 2 MHz for X and 1 MHz for J. The grade also sets how far inductance may move in the soldering heat test."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thicker body for the largest values",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "39 µH, 47 µH, 56 µH and 68 µH use the 1.2 mm thick 201212 body; every smaller value is in the 0.9 mm 201209 body."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Reflow is the intended soldering method. In preheat the ferrite surface may sit no more than 150 ℃ below the solder; on cooling into solvent the part may sit no more than 100 ℃ above it. Insufficient preheat risks surface cracks in the ferrite. Soldering outside the profile risks electrode corrosion, and repeated passes are limited by their summed time. With an iron, the tip must not touch the terminal electrodes."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Keep reels sealed, on shelving, away from sulfur, chlorine, acid fumes, heat shock, vibration and direct sunlight. Parts more than a year past factory inspection, dated by the inspection number on the package, should have their solderability checked before use."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Lead-free",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Parts whose label reads \"Unitary lead free\" meet RoHS. No ozone-depleting substances such as CCl4 or HCFC are used in manufacture."
  }
 ],
